{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue #31:\n- \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n- \"npm test passes\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Right now loadConfig is `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`, so a parse error goes straight up to the caller.\n2. In src/config.js, change loadConfig(path) so that a JSON.parse failure is caught and the function returns {}. Wrap only the JSON.parse call in the try/catch. Do not wrap readFileSync, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as it does now. Keep the export name and the single `path` parameter as they are. Valid JSON must still come back parsed exactly as before.\n3. In test/config.test.js, the test 'bad JSON throws' expects a SyntaxError. That no longer matches the new behaviour. Replace it with a test that `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` deep-equals {}. Keep the 'reads a config' test.\n4. src/app.js merges loadConfig's result over DEFAULTS and has a catch that prints `bad config: ...` and exits 1. Once loadConfig returns {}, a bad JSON file will start the app on the defaults. Leave app.js alone unless something needs to change for it to keep working. README.md currently says invalid JSON stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1. Update that sentence to describe the new behaviour: the app starts with the defaults.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure it passes. Then open the pull request and reference issue #31 in the description.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against each expect line of issue #31, in this order. Run every command from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import {mkdtempSync,writeFileSync} from 'node:fs'; import {tmpdir} from 'node:os'; import {join} from 'node:path'; import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; const f=join(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(),'cfg-')),'config.json'); writeFileSync(f,'{port: 9000'); let r; try { r=loadConfig(f); } catch(e) { console.log('THREW', e && e.name, e && e.message); process.exit(0); } console.log('RESULT', JSON.stringify(r), Object.prototype.toString.call(r));\"\nAlso read loadConfig in src/config.js at the head.\nPASS: the output is `RESULT {} [object Object]`, meaning loadConfig returned an empty plain object without throwing, and src/config.js shows loadConfig catching the error that JSON.parse throws.\nFAIL: the output starts with THREW, the result is anything other than an empty object, or loadConfig does not catch the JSON.parse error.\n\nCheck 2: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: the test run reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: any test fails, or the test run errors out.\n\nAdvisory (these do not decide PASS or FAIL):\n- Make sure valid JSON still parses. Write `{\"port\": 9000}` to a temp file the same way as in Check 1. loadConfig should return {port: 9000}.\n- Make sure a missing file still throws rather than returning {}, which would mean only JSON.parse is wrapped. Run loadConfig('/nonexistent/config.json').\n- Make sure the old test 'bad JSON throws' in test/config.test.js was replaced by a test for the {} result, not just deleted.\n- Run `node src/app.js` against a file containing `{port: 9000`. It should print `listening on 8080` and exit 0. README.md should no longer say that invalid JSON exits with `bad config`."}